WEBVTT

00:00:02.476 --> 00:00:07.474
JHH - Computex Keynote - 6.1.26
________________

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.560
[INTRO VIDEO]
This is how intelligence is

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:22.000
made. A new kind of factory. Generator 
of tokens. The building blocks of AI.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:36.960
Tokens open a new frontier, turning data 
into knowledge, reason, action. They

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:51.040
reveal patterns in complexity you could never 
see. They guard our cities to keep us safe,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:18.520
and lift us high above them. Tokens help 
robots learn from us, work alongside us —

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:45.680
we go where they cannot. Closing the gap between 
hurt and healing, so they’ll breathe easier. And

00:01:45.680 --> 00:02:14.240
the smallest hearts beat stronger. Tokens are 
helping us break new frontiers never attempted,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:43.600
so we can reach our goal. To infinity… and 
beyond… Together. Take the next great leap

00:02:43.600 --> 00:03:09.120
into a bright future for all mankind. And 
here, in Taipei, is where it all begins.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.800
________________
[JANINE]

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.160
Welcome to the stage, NVIDIA founder and CEO,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:29.431
Jensen Huang.
________________

00:03:29.431 --> 00:03:31.200
[JENSEN]
Welcome to GTC,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:42.160
Taiwan. So great to see all of you. Very good to 
be home. I brought my parents home. Where are my

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:55.680
parents? Everybody give a round of applause 
to my mom and dad. And a round of applause

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:07.200
for our pre-game show superstars, ladies 
and gentlemen. Look how adorable they are.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.120
The superstars of Taiwan.
There are so many of you here today. We

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:23.280
are broadcasting this right now to 70 other launch 
parties across Taiwan. 70 different conferences

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.280
are going at the same time. Everybody is watching 
this live. We have so much to tell you, and I

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:36.880
have so many partners to thank. It is incredible 
how large our ecosystem in Taiwan has become.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.560
Most of the time, when people think about 
ecosystem, they think about our software stack.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.240
They think about the developer ecosystem above 
the computing systems that NVIDIA builds. But

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:56.080
NVIDIA’s ecosystem spans all the way upstream 
to all of our supply chain here in Taiwan,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:03.520
where it all begins, and downstream all the way 
to data centers, and eventually to end users.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.440
Today we’re going to talk about almost all of 
the ecosystem. There are so many people to thank.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:32.720
I love my ecosystem here. So many incredible 
companies, some of my favorite ecosystem partners.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:02.880
Taiwan’s rich ecosystem — the world’s best 
supply chain ecosystem. Unbelievable.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.960
Well, thank you all for being here. And 
this year, our businesses together are

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:16.640
growing incredibly. In fact, somebody told me 
last night that the annual GDP of Taiwan is

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:30.960
going to grow almost 10%. Unbelievable. Well, 
we have a lot to talk about. Let’s get going.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.960
Two years ago, when I was here, I started to talk 
to you about how AI has moved from generative AI,

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:43.040
and other waves of AI that are coming. 
The next wave of AI was agentic AI. And

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.600
today we can say that agentic AI has 
arrived, that useful AI has arrived.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.000
Now, what does this mean? This 
is GitHub. This is, of course,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.720
one of the first applications 
of agentic AI — software coding.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:04.800
One of the most valuable professions, an 
incredibly large ecosystem — 30 million,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:11.440
40 million professional software developers, 
probably another couple hundred million who are

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:17.840
students and enthusiasts and so on and so forth. 
But say 30, 40 million software developers in the

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:26.080
world code for a living. And this represents 
most of them. This is GitHub. The pull request

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:33.040
is when they download software, they modify it, 
and commit is when they push it back up. And so,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:44.560
if you could look at this — in 2023 the 
number of commits was 300 million, in 2024,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:59.840
400 million, in 2025, 500 million commits. In the 
first few months of 2026, it has nearly tripled.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:09.440
Now what does that mean? 30 million software 
developers representing about $3 trillion worth

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:18.880
of GDP — that is what they’re paid, $3 trillion 
worth of salaries per year, which is generating

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:25.200
economic growth for the rest of the industries. 
Say $100 trillion of the world’s industries is

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:37.040
impacted — generated by $3 trillion worth of 
salary. That $3 trillion worth of salary is

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:48.960
now producing nearly three times as much output. 
It’s effectively $9 trillion of productivity from

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:56.160
$3 trillion of salaries. Does that make any sense? 
The difference is absolutely extraordinary. This

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.440
is the potential. This is the promise of AI.
The number of software engineers is actually

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:08.320
increasing. People talk about AI reducing 
jobs — complete nonsense. It’s causing more

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.760
software engineers to be hired, and 
the reason for that is very simple.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:20.080
If you can hire a software engineer and you can 
generate $9 trillion worth of productive work,

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:27.920
why wouldn’t you want to hire more software 
engineers? If that line was flat, then obviously

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.640
people will hire fewer software engineers. 
But because the output is so incredible,

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:38.000
people want to hire more software engineers. This 
is going to show up in our economy very soon.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.960
And so, the first thing is: useful AI has arrived. 
Now, what does that mean from the industry’s

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:51.920
perspective? That means that tokens are now in 
extraordinary demand. Because if you can do this,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:57.920
you’re going to want to produce more of it. And 
because tokens are now profitable units — tokens

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:06.400
are now profitable units of revenue. Because it is 
now profitable, the AI companies want to build a

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:12.400
lot more tokens, generate a lot more tokens, build 
more AI factories, which is the reason why compute

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:20.560
demand here in Taiwan has skyrocketed. It is 
precisely the reason why all of you are so busy,

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:28.640
and your businesses are doing so well. In fact, 
that looks like some of your stock prices.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:41.200
The compute pattern has changed. Everything 
has changed. So the first idea is that useful

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:50.480
AI has arrived. AI is now a profit generator. 
AI is now a GDP generator. Behind it is a whole

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.400
new kind of computing pattern — not just a 
large language model, but an agent. Today,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.920
almost everything we’re going to talk 
about is going to be based on this.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:08.160
So let me take a quick moment and show you what 
I’m talking about. This is an agent — an agent

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:15.920
application. In the old days, this would 
be an application — this would be code,

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:23.600
and this would be an operating system. 
Application, code running inside an application,

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:31.680
inside an operating system. Today, it is an agent, 
which consists of a large language model, or many,

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:39.920
sitting inside a harness, and that harness 
helps orchestrate it to do productive work.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:47.760
This is the input — when that input comes, 
it has to understand, observe, reason, act,

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:56.000
use tools. That tool could be a spreadsheet, a 
web browser, a data processing engine, a database

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:05.440
engine, for example. This is orchestrated — this 
harness orchestrates this routing of information.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:12.480
Every single time it touches — either processing 
the context, understanding what is happening,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:22.560
reasoning about what to do, coming up with a plan 
that it acts on — that orchestration is managed by

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:30.560
software. And so this is fundamentally an agent. 
It deals with short-term memory — called working

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:36.560
memory — and long-term memory, just like we 
do. And so the memory management system is

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:45.440
incredibly important. This entire system is called 
an agent. The large language model is used to do

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:52.800
the thinking, and the harness connects everything 
together, just like an operating system.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.880
And so this is the new computing model. 
This is what an agent can do — incredible

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:05.120
things. This is the big breakthrough: 
the simultaneous convergence of large

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:09.920
language models that are now able to do 
a really good job thinking, reasoning,

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:16.240
planning, using tools, and the fact that we 
now have these harnesses that manage memory,

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:22.080
manage orchestration, use tools. We can now do 
amazing things. Let me give you some examples.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:41.120
This is a prompt. This is the code that is 
generated, and this comes out. You guys — what

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:50.400
do you think? Pretty amazing, right? Look, we use 
Claude Code here, but Codex does an incredible

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:57.040
job as well. Here’s another example. This is the 
input: “Create a GIF — NVIDIA green dots on black,

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:08.720
scatter, form Taiwan 101 building, NVLink GTC 
Taipei 2026, morph to NVIDIA logo, then scatter,

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:14.640
repeat.” Right? So you saw that — that was the 
prompt. Here is the next one: “I lost my remote

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:21.920
control battery clip. It looks like this. Create 
a CAD file.” It uses a tool, creates a CAD file,

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:30.320
ready for 3D printing to create a new one. Makes 
sense? This is now the new computing pattern.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:39.920
Whereas we used to launch an application, click 
and type, we now replace that with explaining

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:47.440
to the AI what we want — our intent — and 
the AI generates the code, or uses tools,

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:54.640
and produces the necessary output. This is 
how computers are going to work in the future.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:15:01.200
This is agentic AI. For two years we’ve been 
building towards this, and now it has arrived.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:07.840
Now, one of the big breakthroughs of course is 
tool use. A lot of people have said, “Jensen,

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.080
agentic AI is coming, therefore all of the 
software companies are going to go out of

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:19.280
business.” I said: it’s exactly the opposite. 
Because there are going to be so many agents,

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:25.520
the world is no longer limited by the number 
of people. Therefore, those agents are going

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:32.320
to use more tools than ever. This is actually 
an incredible time to be a software company.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:38.960
But the software has to be presented to the 
agent in a way that the agent can use it.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:46.480
This is a big breakthrough, and in fact, what 
we have done — what NVIDIA’s treasure is — is

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:53.280
all of our CUDA libraries. I call them CUDA-X 
libraries. This is NVIDIA’s treasure. Today,

00:15:53.280 --> 00:16:02.080
we are able to now present these CUDA-X libraries 
to agents who can use them much more effectively

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:11.440
than even humans. And so, this is a wonderful 
time for CUDA-X libraries. Just take a look. 20

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:19.280
years ago we built CUDA, a single architecture for 
accelerated computing. We reinvented computing.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:24.880
A thousand CUDA-X libraries help developers 
make breakthroughs in every field of science

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:34.880
and engineering. CUDA-X libraries are tools for 
agents. cuLitho for computational lithography.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:45.920
cuOpt for decision optimization. cuDSS for direct 
sparse solvers. AI-Q for deep research across

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:57.600
structured and unstructured documents. Aerial for 
AI RAN. PhysicsNeMo for differentiable physics.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:15.200
Parabricks for genomics. At their foundation 
are algorithms, and they are beautiful.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:11.760
A round of applause for math — math is beautiful.
The computing pattern of software is going to

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:22.080
change. In fact, let’s come back to this. This 
is the agent — the ultimate disaggregated and

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:29.360
distributed computing model. So many different 
computers are going to be activated in order to

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:43.520
process this agent. The agent consists of model, 
harness, tools, and skills, and a runtime. All

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:52.240
of that is running at different places in a data 
center. You can think of the model as the brain,

00:20:52.240 --> 00:21:01.280
the harness as the body, and the tools that 
it uses, working in a runtime — think of it

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:08.800
as a workshop. So this is a person, a worker, 
working with tools in a workshop. Of course,

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:14.720
this is being done at extraordinarily large 
scales. And each one of those steps is running

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:19.920
in a different part of the computer.
You can see the large language model is

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:27.760
thinking — context processing, observing, 
understanding the environment, reasoning,

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:34.320
coming up with a plan, and acting on the plan. 
Every single time that happens, an entire rack

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:41.840
of Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 is activated. 
It’s thinking with a large language model.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:50.640
Whenever it uses a tool, a CPU is used. That tool 
could be a C compiler. It could be Python. It

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:58.560
could be JavaScript. Or it could be accelerated 
computing. Today’s agents are relatively simple

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:03.680
users of tools. Tomorrow, they’re going 
to be very sophisticated users of tools,

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:08.160
which is the reason why the CUDA-X libraries 
that I showed you are going to be incredibly

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:14.000
popular with agents. They solve some of the most 
important problems the world knows. And all of

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:23.120
our CUDA-X libraries are now going to come with 
skills that the AI could learn how to use. So the

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:29.360
CUDA-X library comes with skills — basically 
a manual — the AI reads it and goes, “Aha,

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:36.880
that’s how you use it.” The ability to use these 
libraries by agents is going to be incredible.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:44.080
And so, the tools run on CPUs and GPUs and 
large language models. The security harness

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:52.560
runs on CPUs and a security processor called the 
DPU — NVIDIA’s BlueField. The orchestration of all

00:22:52.560 --> 00:23:00.240
this runs on a CPU. This is the entire harness — 
the CPUs orchestrating all of the work. One of the

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:06.800
hardest parts is memory. You can just imagine: the 
working memory — called KV caching — is about what

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:13.840
to remember. Compression, not just compression, 
but how to retrieve. Do you retrieve structured

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:20.320
data? Do you retrieve unstructured data? What is 
the ontology, the relationship of all of these

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:26.560
different data structures to itself? That entire 
processing is incredibly complicated. The memory

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:34.640
system of AI is going to cause the storage 
system to be completely revolutionized.

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:42.000
As you can see, every aspect of this 
computing model — this computing pattern,

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:46.880
this new application called an agent — is 
fundamentally different than the way that

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:52.240
applications used to run: a whole bunch of 
software sitting inside a binary, sitting

00:23:52.240 --> 00:24:00.480
inside an operating system. This is the reason 
— this disaggregated, this distributed, this

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:10.000
heterogeneous computing problem — is precisely the 
reason we built our next generation: Vera Rubin.

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:17.520
Vera Rubin is not one chip. Vera Rubin 
is not a GPU only. It starts with a GPU,

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:28.720
but Vera Rubin is incredible. This entire 
thing is Vera Rubin. From end to end, it has

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:36.800
GPUs — Vera Rubin NVL72. It is orchestrated by 
Vera CPUs that I’m going to tell you more about.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:45.360
The storage systems — revolutionary. Vera, along 
with ConnectX-9, our software stack called DOCA,

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:51.360
the security processor that’s inside so 
that everything is encrypted at rest,

00:24:52.400 --> 00:25:00.160
in motion, as well as in use. Everything 
across this is secure because the AI model

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:07.600
is so precious. This is the reason why this 
entire system observes confidential computing.

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:14.560
Each one of these systems would be a complete 
revolution in itself. Vera Rubin is the most

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:21.760
ambitious endeavor in the history of our company. 
The whole company worked on Vera Rubin — across

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:29.280
all 40,000 engineers, not to mention all of you. 
All of you participated in the creation of this

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:36.720
entire system. Vera Rubin is really a miracle, 
and it’s not just one chip — it is so many.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:43.760
Well, it’s even beyond that. A long time ago, 
NVIDIA used to be a GPU company. But over the

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:50.152
years we’ve evolved to become a systems company. 
You’re looking here now at the most complex

00:25:50.152 --> 00:25:59.280
system, the most complex ground-up system ever 
designed. But ultimately, our customers and our

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:06.720
partners don’t want to buy computers. They want 
to build AI factories, which is the reason why

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:13.760
NVIDIA has really started to transform ourselves 
yet again. You can see so much of our technology

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:20.960
is now at the entire infrastructure scale. Our 
partners are at infrastructure scale — power

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:29.200
generators, cooling systems, the grid providers. 
So many industrial companies are now part of our

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:35.760
ecosystem, because ultimately we’re trying to 
build an entire stack — just like GPUs, just

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:43.040
like when we were building Grace Blackwell NVLink 
72, just like now, we are building a full-stack

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:49.680
system so that our customers could build 
amazing AI infrastructure. Let’s take a look.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.080
________________
[VIDEO NARRATION]

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:56.800
The world is racing to build AI factories 
— the largest infrastructure buildout in

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:04.320
human history. AI factories are incredibly 
complex. Every layer — chip, rack, network,

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:13.680
power, cooling, grid — must be designed together 
from end to end, because compute is revenue.

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:20.000
NVIDIA DSX is the blueprint: a reference design 
for building and operating AI factories at

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:27.600
maximum efficiency and profitability.
It starts with DSX Sim — the Omniverse

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:32.640
blueprint. Partners design and validate an 
NVIDIA Vera Rubin AI factory before a single

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:44.800
rack is ordered. They plan the layout, simulate 
the power and cooling, design the network,

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:52.560
validate every integration, test every change 
in the digital twin. The factory powers on.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:59.520
DSX OS takes over — it provisions, operates, 
monitors, and remediates the infrastructure,

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:08.320
turning the installed systems into trusted, 
multi-tenant, resilient, AI-ready capacity.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:15.040
Today’s AI factories overprovision power 
by up to 40%. DSX MaxLPS lets operators

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:21.760
safely deploy more GPUs inside the same power 
budget, adding billions in annual revenue.

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:28.960
Breakthrough hot liquid cooling 
at 45 degrees Celsius uses less

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:36.240
water and energy — more power going to 
revenue-generating compute. Incredible.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:41.600
Dynamic power allocation steers power from 
rack to rack, recovering stranded watts,

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:48.480
sending them where work is happening. In-rack 
power smoothing flattens peak current spikes

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:57.920
and power surges. Throughout the factory, 
teams of AI agents work with DSX MaxLPS,

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:03.520
continuously coordinating to balance 
cooling and power to meet workload demand.

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:10.160
DSX AI factories are flexible energy assets 
that operate cooperatively with the grid. DSX

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:20.160
Flex reads real-time grid signals and dynamically 
adjusts back to power when the grid needs relief.

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:26.480
100 gigawatts of AI factories will come online 
before the end of the decade. NVIDIA DSX AI

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:36.960
factories run at highest efficiency, produce the 
lowest-cost tokens, and make the grid stronger.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:41.680
________________
[JENSEN]

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:48.800
I’ve shown you ecosystem slides of the past, 
where NVIDIA’s computing layers and software

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:55.680
stacks are integrated into other people’s 
platforms — third-party platforms and libraries

00:29:55.680 --> 00:30:03.520
that serve end markets. That was a computing 
ecosystem. This is an AI factory ecosystem.

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:11.360
This is way downstream of all of you. Upstream 
of me is all of you, and downstream of us is this

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:19.280
ecosystem. Because NVIDIA, ultimately, is not just 
building a GPU, not just building a system — we’re

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:26.320
helping customers build these AI factories, this 
AI infrastructure, that is so immensely complex.

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:34.960
Each one of these, at one gigawatt level, 
started at $20, $30 billion. It is at $50,

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:45.040
$60 billion, and soon it will be $80, $100 
billion per gigawatt. $100 billion into an

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:52.000
AI factory. It must work the first time, and 
it must work right away. The cost of capital

