00:51:44.720 --> 00:51:54.560
Okay, let’s talk about CPUs. Vera CPUs. 
CPUs built for the age of agentic AI.

00:51:54.560 --> 00:52:06.800
All of the CPUs until now were created for people. 
We were the users, we were the renters. The way

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:15.200
we use CPUs — we live in a world counted by 
seconds. The way we rent CPUs in the cloud,

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:24.160
the more CPU cores you have, the more you can 
rent. The economics and the use case of the

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:35.120
old CPU are fundamentally different from agents.
Agents are impatient. They don’t live in a world

00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:42.000
that is counted in seconds. They live in a world 
counted in nanoseconds. When an agent uses a tool,

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:47.760
it wants the response time to be as fast as 
possible. When it accesses a database, it has

00:52:47.760 --> 00:52:55.200
to come back as soon as possible. Every moment 
that the agent is waiting keeps it from going

00:52:55.200 --> 00:53:04.000
to the next step, the next step, the next step. 
It is vital that we make the CPUs as low-latency

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:12.160
as possible, as interactive as possible. 
So we created Vera CPU for the age of AI.

00:53:12.160 --> 00:53:17.920
Now inside our system, it’s used in three 
different ways. The first way, of course,

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:26.960
is within Vera Rubin. For thinking — and inside 
the Vera Rubin rack, there are already two

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:36.800
CPUs. As you know, we are building and selling 
millions of Vera Rubins. We have sold millions

00:53:36.800 --> 00:53:42.240
of Grace Blackwells. And NVIDIA already is 
one of the largest CPU makers in the world.

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:51.280
In the Vera Rubin rack there are two CPUs. 
One for orchestrating and managing the GPUs,

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:59.200
managing the KV cache, dealing with all of the 
software that runs in the rack. We also have

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:08.080
the Grace BlueField that is used for security 
and isolation. The Vera compute is used for

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:16.080
the harness, the orchestration of the AI models, 
tool use, accessing the database. And the data

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:26.880
servers are right here — Vera BlueField — the 
fastest storage servers the world has ever made.

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:35.200
And the reason why this is so vital is because 
agents are accessing memory so incredibly fast.

00:54:35.200 --> 00:54:44.000
These systems — the storage server and the 
CPUs — are in the critical path of the most

00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:51.440
expensive part of the data center. This is 
the most expensive for a good reason. The

00:54:51.440 --> 00:55:01.280
economics of the AI factory are tokens. And the 
tokens are created here. And so, of course, you

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:06.880
want to manufacture and generate as many tokens 
as possible. This is where you put all of your

00:55:06.880 --> 00:55:15.280
economics, and this has to not be in the way.
And so Vera CPU has great requirements on the

00:55:15.280 --> 00:55:20.400
CPU architecture, which is the reason why 
we built a brand new architecture from the

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:29.040
ground up. A CPU the world has never seen 
before. We call it Vera. This is CPU for

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:37.680
agents. All the CPUs of the past, we built 
for humans. This CPU is built for agents.

00:55:37.680 --> 00:55:43.760
There are four things to keep in mind — 
the four takeaways. The first takeaway

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:49.200
is that the instructions per clock 
of Vera has to be incredibly good,

00:55:49.200 --> 00:55:56.320
because we need the latency to be short. We need 
the processing time — single-threaded performance,

00:55:56.320 --> 00:56:03.920
not throughput — single-threaded performance has 
to be world class. Absolutely the best. Which is

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:09.920
the reason why the IPC, the instructions 
per clock of Vera, is so high. It’s the

00:56:09.920 --> 00:56:17.920
highest in the world. 10 instructions fetched, 
decoded, and executed per clock. Number one.

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:24.960
Number two, the bandwidth necessary to 
move data in and out of the CPU has to

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:34.320
be utterly world class — bandwidth per core. 
The third is just bandwidth, period. We’re

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:41.760
moving data — and remember, I said earlier, 
agentic systems are fundamentally disaggregated

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:49.840
and distributed. Disaggregated and distributed. 
When computing is disaggregated and distributed,

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:55.200
networking becomes the problem. Therefore, 
we have to move the data around as fast as

00:56:55.200 --> 00:57:02.560
possible — between the CPU cores, between the 
CPU and the storage, the CPU and the GPU. The

00:57:02.560 --> 00:57:09.440
bandwidth around the system, and inside the 
CPU core, has to be utterly world class.

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:15.360
This is the first CPU that’s been built in a 
long time that is literally at radical limits,

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:22.960
with a fabric that connects all of the CPU 
cores at the speed of light — 3.6 terabytes

00:57:22.960 --> 00:57:34.560
per second. No trip boundary crossings, because 
all of the CPU cores are talking to each other

00:57:34.560 --> 00:57:42.160
with extremely high bandwidth. They’re not rented 
core by core. They’re all working together. The

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:48.400
cross-sectional bandwidth of Vera is off the 
charts. It’s the first CPU to use PCIe Gen 6.

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:59.120
It is also the first to use LPDDR5 with 1.2 
terabytes per second — two to three times the

00:57:59.120 --> 00:58:06.800
bandwidth of the highest-performance CPUs in the 
market. Three times the bandwidth on the inside.

00:58:06.800 --> 00:58:16.720
The bandwidth per core, and the bandwidth 
overall, is world class. Now, remember — the

00:58:16.720 --> 00:58:24.240
number of CPUs is going to be quite high. And 
the reason for that is very simple. We created

00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:33.920
CPUs for humans in the past, and humans — there 
are only a billion of us. There will be billions

00:58:33.920 --> 00:58:41.920
of agents. And these agents are going to be using 
the CPUs with very little patience, because the

00:58:41.920 --> 00:58:50.160
cost of the GPU that they sit next to is too 
high, too valuable, too precious. Therefore,

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:59.200
these CPUs are going to be both performant, but 
they also have to be extremely energy-efficient,

00:58:59.200 --> 00:59:07.520
so that we can pack as much CPU as we can into the 
factory without taking away power from the token

00:59:07.520 --> 00:59:13.760
generation, which we know is how we make money.
These four properties — instructions per clock,

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:21.040
or single-threaded performance; bandwidth per 
core; the total bandwidth around the chip and

00:59:21.040 --> 00:59:28.720
inside the chip; and energy efficiency — define 
Vera. It is absolutely world class. When you

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:35.680
compare it to the highest-performance x86, it is 
just off the charts. When you compare it in real,

00:59:35.680 --> 00:59:44.240
single-threaded performance — real performance 
— it’s off the charts. It is incredible to be

00:59:44.240 --> 00:59:51.440
able to deliver a 5% improvement on CPUs. It is 
incredible to be able to deliver 10%. But this

00:59:51.440 --> 01:00:01.680
kind of performance speedup is just unheard 
of. This is NVIDIA Vera. What do you think?

