the same. And of course, in every single case,

01:47:06.960 --> 01:47:15.760
we build everything completely. We built 
everything vertically, completely integrated

01:47:15.760 --> 01:47:20.480
with co-design, extreme co-design. And 
then when we open it up for everybody

01:47:20.480 --> 01:47:26.800
to use — whichever part you like, whatever 
you want to use, we even help you modify.

01:47:26.800 --> 01:47:33.200
But the one thing that is missing is we need 
a reference platform for robotic systems.

01:47:33.200 --> 01:47:37.680
These robotic systems are so complicated 
— so many motors, so many sensors,

01:47:37.680 --> 01:47:44.320
so fragile — and yet we need to have a way to 
deliver these reference platforms, just like we

01:47:44.320 --> 01:47:51.520
do with PCs and DGXs and clouds and self-driving 
cars. We now are going to do it for robots. Today,

01:47:51.520 --> 01:47:59.600
we’re announcing the NVIDIA Isaac GR00T reference 
humanoid robot — fully integrated. 25 degrees of

01:47:59.600 --> 01:48:09.040
freedom on each hand, made by Sharpa. 31 degrees 
of freedom on the robot, 6 feet, 150 pounds. Just

01:48:09.040 --> 01:48:20.720
like me. The first number is shorter, the second 
number is bigger. Otherwise, pretty close.

01:48:20.720 --> 01:48:27.520
And this platform runs the new Jetson Thor and 
our entire software stack — data generation stack,

01:48:27.520 --> 01:48:34.320
data simulation stack, the runtime — 
all integrated into a robot designed

01:48:34.320 --> 01:48:40.160
for everyone to use. We built this for 
higher education and university researchers,

01:48:40.160 --> 01:48:45.600
because for them to build this themselves is 
insanely hard. And so let’s take a look at that.

01:48:45.600 --> 01:48:46.320
________________
[VIDEO NARRATION]

01:48:46.320 --> 01:48:51.680
The next leap in AI is general-purpose 
robots — humanoids. But building one is

01:48:51.680 --> 01:48:57.360
hard. Every team starts from scratch, stitching 
together simulators, teleoperation systems,

01:48:57.360 --> 01:49:03.360
data pipelines, and training infrastructure. 
Months of setup before research can start.

01:49:03.360 --> 01:49:10.320
NVIDIA Isaac GR00T — an open development 
platform for humanoid robots. Open models,

01:49:10.320 --> 01:49:17.360
simulation and training libraries, and data 
generators. Plus, the robot computer — fully

01:49:17.360 --> 01:49:22.240
pre-configured, ready to go in hours.
First, set up the simulation environment

01:49:22.240 --> 01:49:31.680
in Isaac Lab. Capture demonstrations with 
Isaac Teleoperation on a real or simulated

01:49:31.680 --> 01:49:42.240
robot. Generate synthetic data with Omniverse and 
Cosmos, scaling one demonstration into thousands.

01:49:42.240 --> 01:50:03.600
Train policies, evaluate them in Isaac Lab Arena. 
Deploy through Isaac ROS, running on Jetson Thor.

01:50:03.600 --> 01:50:12.480
Every element is modular and open — use ours 
or swap in your own. GR00T is powering robotics

01:50:12.480 --> 01:50:20.160
research across every discipline for every 
domain, from research labs to factory floors.

01:50:20.160 --> 01:50:34.480
One open platform. And now, a new edition: Isaac 
GR00T reference design robots — built on NVIDIA’s

01:50:34.480 --> 01:50:42.000
open platform, ready for frontier research, for 
any lab, anywhere. The age of robotics starts

01:50:42.000 --> 01:50:48.791
here. NVIDIA Isaac GR00T.
